From 8087e90aabc7d54805edee7a174eb2ade5aa745b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Paul Lamb <paul@paulscode.com>
Date: Sat, 19 Sep 2026 22:25:11 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 3/4] doc: the chain identity, with the invoice prefix
 withdrawn

Records what separates the two rule sets now that chain_hash is shared and the
BOLT 11 prefix is not changing either: the even bit at init, the gossip floor
at the activation height, option_unified_sigs in channel_type, and
option_blake2b in the BOLT 11 and BOLT 12 feature fields.

The last of those is specified and not yet built, on either side, and the
document says so rather than describing an intention as a fact. With the prefix
unchanged and the bit not yet set, an invoice minted on this chain is
indistinguishable from one minted on the earlier rules, and the only thing
preventing a cross-chain payment is that the two graphs do not meet. That is a
weaker guarantee than an explicit refusal and it is written down as such.

Also records that the BOLT 11 even-bit check is load-bearing and that both
implementations ship a decode path which skips it, Core Lightning for its cli
tool and lnd by default. Neither is a bug today and both become one when an
invoice's chain depends on the bit.

The feature-bit sections say where the numbers are going, so that a reader of
"bit 68" is not left thinking it is settled.
